{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"aed5dcb456de2517","name":"card_event_jungle_warfare","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Japan/BrothersInArms/events/card_event_jungle_warfare","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"JUNGLE WARFARE","text":"Give a unit: \"Destruction: Deal 2 damage to a random enemy.\" Trigger its Destruction effects.","flavorText":"During WWII, the Japanese forces were generally considered to have an advantage over the Allies in jungle warfare. The Japanese had extensive experience fighting in jungles due to their prior military campaigns in China and Southeast Asia, which gave them a deep understanding of the terrain and tactics suited for jungle warfare. They employed guerrilla tactics, utilized camouflage effectively, and were skilled in navigating the dense vegetation. They excelled at ambushes, infiltrating enemy positions, and conducting surprise attacks. In contrast, the Allied forces initially faced challenges adapting to the unique demands of jungle warfare. They lacked experience and training specific to these environments, and the dense foliage, limited visibility, difficult terrain, and tropical diseases posed significant obstacles. However, as the war progressed, the Allies learned valuable lessons and developed effective countermeasures. The Allies improved their jungle training, equipment, and tactics. They utilized superior firepower, air support, and artillery to offset the Japanese advantages. They also developed techniques for jungle patrolling, anti-guerrilla operations, and utilizing native guides for intelligence gathering. Over time, the Allies adapted and developed successful strategies to combat the Japanese in jungle environments. Campaigns such as Guadalcanal, New Guinea, and Burma showcased the Allies growing proficiency in jungle warfare and their ability to overcome Japanese resistance.","faction":"Japan","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":4,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaul